Subject: [PATCH 4/5] dma: 32 KB buffers, so a batch can be four times as big

The ring is DMA_BUFFER_COUNT * DMA_BUFFER_SIZE and it is what bounds a bcrypt batch: the bridge asks
the transport how much it will take at once and divides by its own worst-case record to get the
candidate ceiling for a launch. At 256 x 8192 that is 2 MB, which is 26,214 candidates of the packed
password record and was 16,384 of the fixed 128-byte one it replaced.

THE CANDIDATE CEILING IS A THROUGHPUT NUMBER, NOT A BUFFER SIZE. hashcat drives the card in one-shot
batches: arm, send, wait, drain, disarm. Every batch therefore fills and drains the core array once,
and on a 450-core board that fixed cost is one core-period per launch however big the launch is.
Measured on hardware, 16,200 candidates is 36 periods of work in 37 periods of time, so 2.7 percent
is lost to it; 104,857 candidates is 233 in 234, which is 0.43. The array is not idle for longer with
a bigger ring, it is idle the same amount less often.

Raising the COUNT is not available: the descriptor table in the gateware is a 256-entry FIFO and the
driver fills exactly that many, so the two numbers are one number and changing it means rebuilding
every bitstream. Raising the SIZE needs no gateware change at all. The descriptor length field is 24
bits, so 32 KB is far inside it, and nothing in the fabric has cared about the buffer size since the
host record format stopped being a fixed 128 bytes: the feeder used to check its word count against
the buffer-end marker, and it now checks a tag in every record header instead.

32 KB rather than 64 KB. Each buffer is a separate dma_alloc_coherent, so the size is also the
contiguous allocation order the host has to satisfy 512 times at probe: 32 KB is order 3 and 64 KB is
order 4, and the extra headroom is not worth the higher chance of a probe failing on a fragmented
machine. 8 MB a direction, 16 MB a device.

Signed-off-by: See docs/credits.txt

--- a/config.h
+++ b/config.h
@@ -76,7 +76,7 @@
 #define DMA_CHANNEL_COUNT      DMA_CHANNELS
 #define DMA_BUFFER_PER_IRQ     32
 #define DMA_BUFFER_COUNT       256
-#define DMA_BUFFER_SIZE        8192
+#define DMA_BUFFER_SIZE        32768
 #define DMA_BUFFER_TOTAL_SIZE (DMA_BUFFER_COUNT*DMA_BUFFER_SIZE)
 //#define DMA_BUFFER_ALIGNED
 
